solution_steps:
Rationalizing the denominators, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}+1} + \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}-1}} &= \frac{1}{\frac{\sqrt{2}-1}{(\sqrt{2}+1)(\sqrt{2}-1)} + \frac{2(\sqrt{3}+1)}{(\sqrt{3}-1)(\sqrt{3}+1)}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{\sqrt{2}-1}{1} + \frac{2(\sqrt{3}+1)}{2}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}-1 + \sqrt{3}+1} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3}}.
\end{align*}
To rationalize the denominator again, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3}} &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3}}{(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})(\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3})} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3}}{2-3} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}-\sqrt{3}}{-1} \\
&= \boxed{\sqrt{3}-\sqrt{2}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \sqrt{3}-\sqrt{2}

